(
    version: 1,
    id: "m02",
    title: "Counting Turns",
    chapter: 1,
    concepts: ["Wheel kinematics", "Encoders"],
    world: (id: "test_track"),
    robot: Starter("rover"),
    time_limit_s: 10.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.6, 1.0),
    brief_md: r#"
## Drive exactly 750 mm

Stop the Rover so that the **centre of the hub** is at x = 750 mm (the 750 mm position on the
track, halfway between the 700 and the 800 marks). The allowed error is 10 mm.

The battery starts at a different charge on each of the three test runs, and a tired battery makes
the motors slower. A program that only counts time will be wrong on at least one of them.

The wheels are 56 mm across. How many turns make 750 mm?
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "How far does the Rover go when its wheels turn exactly once?",
        unit: "mm",
        measured: "one_turn_mm",
    )),
    criteria: [FinalX(x: 0.75, tol_m: 0.01)],
    stars: (
        (text: "Stop within 3 mm", criteria: [FinalX(x: 0.75, tol_m: 0.003)]),
        (text: "Be there and stopped in under 4 seconds", criteria: [FinalX(x: 0.75, tol_m: 0.01, within_s: Some(4.0))]),
    ),
    hints: (
        "Time is not distance: with a weaker battery the same power gives a slower robot. Ask the wheels how far they turned instead.",
        "One turn of a 56 mm wheel rolls pi times 56 mm = 176 mm. The angle axl_motor_angle(left) is in radians, so the distance is the angle times the wheel radius, 0.028 m.",
        "axl_motor_position(motor, radians, max_speed) turns a motor to an angle by itself and holds it there. Give both motors the angle 0.75 / 0.028.",
    ),
)
